name turtle
# Turtle. Every statement is recorded as written: [subject, predicate, object]. An identifier between angle brackets
# is recorded without them, in whichever of the three places it stands; a quoted text without its quotes and with its
# escapes resolved; a name written with a prefix keeps it; "a" is recorded as the "a" the file writes.
# A text that carries a language tag says so, [text, tag], and one that carries a datatype says that: [text, datatype].
# Not read: statements of a node the file does not name (a blank node), and collections.
format turtle

thing triple subject
thing rdf_literal value
content *
# A statement's predicate is an RDF property: a relation of the RDF vocabulary the file writes, as written (RDF 1.1
# Concepts: "Each triple consists of a subject, a predicate and an object ... the predicate, which is an IRI"; RDF 1.1
# Turtle: "The token 'a' in the predicate position of a Turtle triple represents the IRI
# http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"). A prefixed name, and the a written for rdf:type, are recorded as
# written: their resolution to the IRI they abbreviate is Laplace-Engine#20 (Laplace-Engine#32).
value - predicate
relate property predicate to object_list
pair rdf_literal lang_tag datatype
